1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,140
This is doing and feeling. Episode 3, Measuring. So here's my first question. Can you even do experiments on something that's defined as private?

2
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:17,940
it. You can with a trick. In 1990, Francis Crick of DNA fame and the neuroscientist Christoph Koch

3
00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:24,320
proposed setting the deep philosophical puzzle aside for a while. Instead, they said, hunt for

4
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:29,580
the neural correlates of consciousness. The minimal brain activity, that's enough for a particular

5
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:36,640
conscious experience. The key method is contrast. You keep the stimulus identical, but arrange things

6
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:41,160
so that sometimes the person consciously sees it and sometimes they do not.

7
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,640
Then you compare what the brain is doing in the two cases.

8
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:49,080
How do you make someone not see something that's right in front of them?

9
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:50,740
There are lots of ways.

10
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,000
One is masking.

11
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:56,440
Flash a word for a few hundredths of a second, then follow it with a pattern.

12
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,900
Set the timing right at the threshold,

13
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:02,760
and the very same flash is sometimes seen and sometimes not.

14
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:05,100
Another is binocular rivalry.

15
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:12,500
different image, say a face to one eye and a house to the other. Your experience flips back and forth

16
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:18,120
between face and house, even though the input never changes. And there's inattentional blindness,

17
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:24,400
like the famous video where people count basketball passes and about half of them do not notice a

18
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:30,280
person in a gorilla suit walking through the middle of the scene. In every case, the world stays the

19
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:36,140
same while the experience changes. That's the handle science can grab. And what have those

20
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,480
Some of the most striking findings come from patients.

21
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:42,160
Start with blindsight.

22
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:48,720
Some people with damage to the primary visual cortex say they're completely blind in part of their visual field.

23
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:55,680
Yet when you ask them to guess, they can point to objects there and tell the direction of movement far better than chance.

24
00:01:55,880 --> 00:02:01,700
Lawrence Weisskrantz studied a famous patient known as D.B. in the 1970s.

25
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:05,420
It's the real-world version of the novel Blindsight's question.

26
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:11,800
visual processing, with no visual experience, or at least none the person can report.

27
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:17,500
Then there are split-brain patients whose corpus callosum, the bridge between the two

28
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:24,220
hemispheres, was cut to treat severe epilepsy. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga showed that

29
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:29,380
each hemisphere could respond to information the other one did not have, and the left hemisphere,

30
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:35,380
which controls speech, would confidently invent explanations for actions that the right hemisphere

31
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:38,680
Gazzaniga called this the interpreter.

32
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,700
It's a lasting warning that our explanations of our own behavior

33
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:46,380
can be fluent, confident stories made up after the fact.

34
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:49,200
That seems relevant to AI as well.

35
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:52,300
Very. Hold that thought until episode 4.

36
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:54,900
While we're on brains fooling themselves,

37
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:58,580
Aguera E. Arcus says AI could have free will,

38
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:01,820
but I thought neuroscience had disproved free will.

39
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:06,620
That usually refers to Benjamin Libet's experiments in the early 1980s.

40
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:13,080
He found a slow buildup of electrical activity, called the readiness potential, starting a few

41
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:18,540
hundred milliseconds before people reported deciding to move. It was widely read as the

42
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:24,580
brain deciding before you do. But in 2012, Aaron Scherger and his colleagues showed that the

43
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:29,780
buildup can be explained as random neural noise drifting toward a threshold, with the movement

44
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:35,060
starting when it crosses. On that reading, the signal is not a hidden decision at all.

45
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:42,040
to be careful with dramatic interpretations of single experiments. Then there are patients who

46
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:48,940
seem completely unresponsive. In 2006, Adrienne Owens' group put a young woman diagnosed as

47
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:54,760
vegetative into a brain scanner. They asked her to imagine playing tennis and then to imagine

48
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:59,860
walking through her house. Her brain activity matched that of healthy volunteers doing the

49
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:06,060
same tasks. She could follow instructions, so she was aware. Later studies used the same trick

50
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:08,620
yes or no answers from patients.

51
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:11,560
And a large study published in 2024

52
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,080
found signs of this hidden awareness

53
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:16,180
in roughly one in four patients

54
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:18,920
who could not follow any commands at the bedside.

55
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:22,120
So is there anything like a consciousness meter?

56
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:25,900
The closest thing is the perturbational complexity index

57
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,640
from Marcello Massimini and his colleagues.

58
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:30,980
It's nicknamed zap and zip.

59
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:33,580
You zap the cortex with a magnetic pulse

60
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:35,900
and record the brain's echo with EEG.

61
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:39,720
Then you zip it. You compress the response like compressing a file.

62
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:43,780
In a conscious brain, the echo is both widespread and differentiated.

63
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,960
It's complex and hard to compress.

64
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:50,560
In dreamless sleep under most anesthetics or in most vegetative patients,

65
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:54,060
the echo is either local or a big uniform wave.

66
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:56,180
Either way, it compresses easily.

67
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:59,160
Across sleep, anesthesia, and brain injury,

68
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:03,080
this measure separates conscious from unconscious states remarkably well.

69
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:05,880
It was calibrated on people who could say afterwards

70
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:12,720
had experienced anything. My favorite case is ketamine. At anesthetic doses, it leaves people

71
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:18,280
completely unresponsive, yet they often report vivid dreams when they wake up. And their brains

72
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:23,300
showed high complexity, matching what they reported rather than how they looked from the outside.

73
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:30,260
It works in humans. And just last month, a study across six species, from humans down to roundworms,

74
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:36,100
reported that anesthesia leaves a common signature in all of them. Neural activity becomes less

75
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:39,440
and its past predicts its future less well.

76
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:43,760
But whether measures like these mean anything for an octopus or a computer

77
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:46,860
is exactly the question that theory has to answer.

78
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:49,620
Okay theories, how many are there?

79
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:50,780
Too many.

80
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,880
A 2022 review by Anil Seth and Tim Bain listed 22 of them.

81
00:05:56,260 --> 00:05:58,720
Here are the five families you'll keep running into.

82
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:00,980
One, global workspace theory.

83
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:03,640
Bernard Bars proposed it in 1988,

84
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:08,040
and Stanislas Doan and Jean-Pierre Shanja developed a neural version.

85
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:12,140
The idea is that the brain is a society of specialized processors,

86
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:14,860
mostly working unconsciously and in parallel.

87
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:19,140
Consciousness is what happens when some piece of content wins a competition

88
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:21,640
and gets broadcast across the whole system,

89
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:25,680
so that memory, planning, language, and report can all use it.

90
00:06:26,180 --> 00:06:28,340
Doan's lab found a signature of this.

91
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:30,740
When a stimulus is consciously seen,

92
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:36,380
there's a sudden, all-or-nothing ignition of activity across frontal and parietal networks,

93
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:42,500
roughly a third of a second after it appears. His book, Consciousness and the Brain, is the

94
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:48,340
best introduction. Two, higher-order theories. A mental state is conscious when it's represented

95
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:54,260
by another mental state. In effect, the brain notices that it's seeing. David Rosenthal

96
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:58,960
developed the philosophy, and Hakwin Lau and others developed neuroscience versions

97
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:05,820
by the prefrontal cortex. Steve Fleming's book Know Thyself, about metacognition,

98
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:10,880
is a friendly way in. 3. Recurrent Processing Theory, from Victor Lama.

99
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:16,420
Feedback loops within the sensory cortex are enough for experience, even without a global

100
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:22,600
broadcast. That implies you can have experiences you're unable to report, which is controversial

101
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:28,620
because report is how we normally check. So the theories disagree about where in the brain

102
00:07:28,620 --> 00:07:34,260
consciousness happens. That's one of the sharpest disputes, sometimes called front versus back.

103
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,620
Workspace and higher order theories say the prefrontal cortex is essential.

104
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:45,480
I, I, T, and recurrent processing say the back of the cortex is enough, and that a lot of frontal

105
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:50,660
activity reflects thinking about, and reporting, what you saw, rather than the seeing itself.

106
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:55,360
To pull those apart, researchers use what are called no-report paradigms.

107
00:07:55,360 --> 00:08:03,460
They infer what someone is experiencing from involuntary signals, like eye movements or pupil size, without asking for a report.

108
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:10,040
When the report disappears, some frontal activity disappears with it, which the back camp takes as support.

109
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:13,960
The front camp replies that subtler frontal signals remain.

110
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:16,600
4. Integrated information theory

111
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:27,720
Scientifically, IIT points to a posterior hot zone at the back of the cortex as the core substrate of experience rather than the front, and it makes some strange predictions.

112
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:33,840
For example, neurons that are inactive but could fire still contribute to what you experience.

113
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:38,800
Weighed a lot of scientists called IIT pseudoscience right.

114
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:45,900
They did. In 2023, more than 100 researchers signed an open letter calling it pseudoscience.

115
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:57,720
Their main complaints were that its boldest claims, like attributing consciousness to simple grid-like circuits, cannot really be tested, and that media coverage had overstated the evidence for it.

116
00:08:57,940 --> 00:09:04,320
Many other researchers, including some who do not accept I, IT, thought the label went much too far.

117
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:14,100
In 2025, the fight moved into the journal Nature Neuroscience, where the critics, the theory's defenders, and a third camp calling for calm each had their say.

118
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:16,480
It shows how heated the field can get.

119
00:09:17,060 --> 00:09:18,840
5. Predictive Processing

120
00:09:18,840 --> 00:09:22,340
Anil Seth's book Being You is the best introduction here.

121
00:09:22,620 --> 00:09:28,020
On this view, perception is the brain's best guess about the causes of its sensory signals.

122
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:32,420
Seth calls it a controlled hallucination, controlled by the world.

123
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:35,920
And the deepest layer of prediction is about the body itself,

124
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:38,920
heartbeat, blood chemistry, temperature.

125
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:43,800
Seth argues that emotions, and the most basic feeling of being a self,

126
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:47,260
are rooted in predicting and regulating the body to stay alive.

127
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:49,400
He calls this the beast machine.

128
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:52,220
Mark Soames pushes in a similar direction.

129
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,460
He argues that consciousness starts not in the cortex, but in the brainstem,

130
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:00,760
as raw feeling, signaling how well we're doing at staying alive.

131
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:05,120
And Carl Fristen's free energy principle supplies the mathematics.

132
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:10,840
Living systems persist by minimizing the gap between what they predict and what they sense.

133
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:15,300
The active inference textbook One Reply Recommended is the rigorous version.

134
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:16,580
It's heavy going.

135
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:19,760
One of the papers on your list, A Beautiful Loop,

136
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:24,120
is a recent attempt to build a full theory of consciousness on this framework.

137
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:27,900
Its key idea is what the authors call epistemic depth.

138
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:32,480
The brain's model of the world recursively contains the knowledge that it exists.

139
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:34,280
That's the loop in the title.

140
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:38,820
Is there anything that bridges back to the illusionists from last time?

141
00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:45,840
Yes, Michael Graziano's attention schema theory. The brain controls attention, and to control

142
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:51,460
anything well, you need a model of it. The attention schema is that model. It's simplified,

143
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:56,880
almost cartoonish, and it depicts attention as a kind of inner essence that grasps things.

144
00:10:57,340 --> 00:11:03,460
Graziano argues that this simplified self-model is why we insist we have an indescribable inner

145
00:11:03,460 --> 00:11:08,800
awareness. He also thinks it could be built into machines. His book, Rethinking Consciousness,

146
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:12,680
is short and clear. And the textbook that takes a year.

147
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:19,200
That's Simona, Ginsberg, and Eva, Yablonka's The Evolution of the Sensitive Soul. They propose an

148
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:24,980
evolutionary marker for consciousness, which they call unlimited associative learning. It's the

149
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:29,800
ability to learn about new combinations of stimuli and actions in an open-ended way.

150
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:35,460
They argue it emerged during the Cambrian explosion, more than 500 million years ago,

151
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:40,240
in early vertebrates and arthropods, and later encephalopods like the octopus.

152
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:43,580
That makes it a direct bridge between our two topics.

153
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:48,200
On their view, a particular kind of learning, which is a form of intelligence,

154
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:50,980
is the evolutionary signature of feeling.

155
00:11:51,280 --> 00:11:54,920
With this many theories, how does the field make progress?

156
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:57,680
By making them fight under agreed rules.

157
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:07,840
The flagship example is an adversarial collaboration between global workspace theory and I, I-T, run by a group called the Cogitate Consortium.

158
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:13,980
Leading proponents of both theories agreed in advance on predictions that would embarrass their own theory.

159
00:12:14,180 --> 00:12:16,580
A neutral team then ran the experiments.

160
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:27,520
Hundreds of participants, three different methods, including fMRI, MEG, and recordings from electrodes placed inside the brains of epilepsy patients.

161
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:32,600
The results, published in Nature in 2025, challenged both theories.

162
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:39,100
I.I.T. had predicted sustained synchronization between regions at the back of the brain

163
00:12:39,100 --> 00:12:41,700
lasting as long as a stimulus was seen.

164
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:43,480
That did not show up.

165
00:12:43,700 --> 00:12:48,900
Global workspace theory had predicted a second burst of ignition when a stimulus disappeared,

166
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:53,020
and that the prefrontal cortex would represent details of what was seen,

167
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:55,740
like its exact identity and orientation.

168
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:04,340
not show up either. So both theories lost. Both took real hits, and both camps disputed what the

169
00:13:04,340 --> 00:13:09,460
results mean. But the method is the story. Theories made risky predictions in advance,

170
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:15,120
and some of those predictions failed. That's a field becoming a science. More rounds are underway.

171
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:20,620
A second Cogitate experiment was presented this summer, and another collaboration is pitting I,

172
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:27,300
IT, against active inference. Neither has published a verdict yet. If the theories disagree

173
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:33,400
much. Why should I trust the markers? Because the markers are checked against things, we can verify

174
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:39,680
independently what people report and what they remember when they recover. The theories mostly

175
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:45,660
argue about why the markers work. The front versus back fight is the exception since it's partly a

176
00:13:45,660 --> 00:13:52,020
dispute over whether some frontal signals track seeing or just reporting. Okay, give me the summary.

177
00:14:20,600 --> 00:14:26,320
test bruised the two biggest. And three, every one of those markers was calibrated in humans,

178
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:30,500
where behavior, report, and biology all come bundled together.

179
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:35,220
AI unbundles them. That's the subject of the final episode.

